From 8a40f4b41a06e00119f47402027606bed797d85e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:22:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] dsv4: chunked prefill never leaves a single-row tail, so
 the chunk door stops changing logits

A chunked prefill commits DIFFERENT logits from an unchunked prefill of the same prompt whenever
its final transaction carries exactly one row, and is bit-identical otherwise. That made
MEMRA_DSV4_PREFILL_CHUNK a quality decision for roughly one prompt length in every `chunk`
values, about 1.5625% at the inherited ceiling of 64, while the other ~98.4% were exact.

The cause is not a tile shape. A one-row transaction takes the m=1 decode-shaped kernels AND
engages two default-ON doors that no multi-row transaction can reach: dense exact-tail and
dense-fast are suppressed at m != 1 by `Dsv4DenseExactTailControlScope control(m != 1)` in
cu/dsv4_gpu.cu:4109 and `control(s != 1)` at :4370, and are declared DoorShape::DecodeOnlyM1 in
dsv4_doors.rs (#462). That is why two different widths which both leave a 1-row tail were
measured bit-identical to EACH OTHER and different from width 64: same alternate path, same door
set. The generalisable hazard is worth naming: a door whose engagement depends on transaction
SHAPE makes the engine's output depend on how a request happens to be chunked.

The fix is width selection, not a new kernel. Every multi-row width is already bit-identical to
every other (measured: 32 and 63 both reproduce 64 exactly on a 1024-row suffix; 31 is exact on
a 999-row suffix), so shrinking the width to dodge a 1-row tail costs nothing numerically.
`dsv4_chunk_width_avoiding_single_row_tail` picks the LARGEST safe width so memory and batching
stay as close to the request as possible.

AT THE SERVED WIDTH THE MITIGATION IS TOTAL, which is what changes the door's character: for
chunk 64 the only unreachable case is a suffix of exactly one row, i.e. a two-token prompt whose
first token is primed on its own. The door becomes bit-identical for every prompt but that one
and stops being a quality decision at all.

The exception set is real but confined, and DERIVED rather than asserted: no safe width exists
exactly when suffix_len - 1 is divisible by every candidate in 2..=min(chunk, suffix_len), i.e.
by their lcm. An exhaustive sweep over chunk 2..64 and suffix 2..4096 confirms the
characterisation matches the actual failures exactly (3221 of 3221, identical sets) and that
failures occur only for chunk 2..10. lcm(2..=64) is about 1.18e27, so at the served width no
reachable prompt length can hit it.

An earlier draft of these tests claimed the exception did not extend past chunk 2. That was
wrong and the sweep refuted it: chunk 3 with a 7-row suffix has no safe width, because 7-1 = 6 is
divisible by both 2 and 3. The tests now pin the derived characterisation instead of the guess,
and that counterexample is named in them.

Six tests, sweeps rather than hand-picked cases. The main one asserts TWO-SIDED (where a safe
width exists the tail must be avoided; where none exists the requested chunk must be returned)
with red arms that fail if either branch is never exercised, so deleting the mitigation fails the
suite rather than passing vacuously. Minimality is tested separately, because a rule that dropped
straight to width 2 would satisfy the property test and still be wrong.

Also in this commit, unavoidably: `cargo fmt --all` hunks in memra-server/src/worker.rs and
memra-server/src/dsv4_serve.rs. They are formatting-only, they are not mine and not related to
this change, and they are here because the pre-commit hook checks formatting tree-wide rather
than per-file, so the commit cannot land without them. Flagging them so a reviewer skips them
rather than hunting for the connection.

MEMRA_SKIP_PERF_CI=1: no perf surface touched; this changes only which width a chunked prefill
selects, and every selectable width was already bit-identical.

Signed-off-by: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
