From 5aad4bd3b69cfcc971b0e8ad6fb2c2060849c8cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 01:30:18 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] dsv4: chunked prefill is the naked default and
 MEMRA_DSV4_PREFILL_CHUNK is removed

Under the standing no-OFF-doors ruling a door is the default or it is deleted. This receipt is
positive and categorical, so the door goes and the behaviour stays.

REMOVED: the env read, the resolve_prefill_chunk parser and its four refusal branches, the
chunk == 0 dispatch arm, and the two "requires a nonzero MEMRA_DSV4_PREFILL_CHUNK" errors that
existed only because the flag could be off. The FLAGS.md catalogue row moves to the removed-doors
ledger with the verdict and the receipt pointer. There is no KERNELS.md door row for this flag,
so nothing moves there.

KEPT: the gates and their red arms, which are checks rather than doors, and the boot-time width
bound, which is now a compile-time constant rather than a parse.

THE CASE, categorical rather than a rate: monolithic prefill allocates 673 KiB of scratch per
prompt token and dies on CUDA_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY at 15,974 tokens on a 2x96 GiB pair, 3.4 s in
with no first token, while chunked allocates 7.9 KiB per token and serves 110,554. Chunking also
roughly doubles what fits inside the shipped 90 s deadline. The separation cell measured the
scratch as O(chunk) and independent of prompt length, 0.073 GiB at width 16 against 0.292 GiB at
width 64, exactly 4.00x for 4x the width, which is what this flag claimed to bound.

WHAT THE PROMOTION IS NOT, recorded in the ledger because the lane published the opposite first
and withdrew it: chunked prefill is a NEW NUMERIC CLASS against monolithic at every prompt
length, not for a thin slice. Gate g-r4 compared the programs at n = 65, 100, 129, 256, 513 and
1000, whose tails at width 64 are 0, 35, 0, 63, 0 and 39 so none is a 1-row-tail case, and all
six are bit_equal=no. On those rows the divergence is sub-top-1 (top1_changed=0,
greedy_identical=6/6, KL fwd mean 1.850e-2 max 9.687e-2), which is an encouraging early signal
and is NOT the paired task-accuracy control the HC S16 precedent requires. That control is owed
and tracked in darklanes #457.

The ledger entry also replaces the exactness law the deleted row asserted. "Widths 1..32 use
register-specialized exact twins; 33..64 tile those same exact row kernels by eight" does not
describe the engine. The boundary is one row versus two, because a single-row transaction takes
the m=1 decode-shaped kernels AND engages two doors declared DoorShape::DecodeOnlyM1 (#462), and
the prompt lengths are part of the fact since a width's class depends on the prompt it meets.

TESTS: the flag-parsing module is replaced rather than deleted. What is left to check is that
the served width stays inside the kernel bound, that MEMRA_CTX below the width lowers it without
ever returning zero (the removed door's OFF state reappearing by arithmetic), and a RED ARM that
no chunk value now disables chunking for a long prompt, which the old chunk == 0 dispatch did.
The non-Unicode env test kept its subject by moving to a live flag name, since it is about
env_text rather than about which flag it reads.

MEMRA_SKIP_PERF_CI=1: the served path is the arm that was already measured throughout this lane.

